# Line endings are load-bearing on the Windows engine path.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, and
# actions/checkout does not override it, so a windows-2022 runner checks text
# files out with CRLF unless told otherwise. Three things break when it does:
#
#   1. lock_hash() is sha256(engine/obs.lock), and the artifact identity is the
#      first 12 of it. A CRLF checkout hashes differently from the macOS
#      runner's LF checkout, so the two platforms would name their artifacts
#      with DIFFERENT hash suffixes off the same lock -- silently breaking the
#      one-lock-one-identity property release.yml depends on.
#      (Measured: LF a023c6871ea8, CRLF a6b79a815958.)
#   2. The patchset sha256 in the lock is computed over the .patch bytes, so a
#      CRLF checkout fails patchset verification outright.
#   3. Git Bash runs the build scripts, and bash treats a trailing \r as part of
#      the token: a CRLF script dies with "$'\r': command not found".
#
# Pin the hash-critical and Bash-executed files to LF everywhere.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.patch     text eol=lf
*.lock      text eol=lf
*.cmake     text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
